{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015UBQZMtiZTLqnTcTWv4oEG","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t+++\n2\tid = \"WI-850\"\n3\ttitle = \"A spine row is approved only after the rows it hangs from are\"\n4\tworkstream = \"process\"\n5\tsr_refs = [\"SR-178\"]\n6\tspecref = \"docs/plans/2026-10-07-wi841-retro/PROPOSAL.md\"\n7\tneeds = [\"WI-892\"]\n8\tbuildtier = \"medium\"\n9\tsafety_class = \"ordinary\"\n10\tpriority = 5\n11\tplanmode = \"single\"\n12\t+++\n13\t\n14\t## Context\n15\t\n16\tRe-homed 2026-10-10 (D-015): the shared lane-commit walk this row rides\n17\tmoved from WI-828 (closed partial) to WI-892, which landed the same day.\n18\tOI-113 was ruled (d), won't fix, on 2026-10-14, and WI-893 was cancelled: no\n19\thook walks a hand merge, and the merge slot is the sanctioned landing path.\n20\t\n21\tFiled by hand on 2026-10-07 from the WI-841 retrospective (proposal §7.2,\n22\trow P3), carrying the owner's ruling 4 of 2026-10-07\n23\t(`docs/log.d/2026-10-07-wi841-retro-owner-rulings.md`): a child is approved\n24\tonly after its parent. The owner expected the check to exist. It does not:\n25\tLLR-281 and TC-291 have been Approved under SR-224, which is still `Drafted`,\n26\tsince WI-615 (2026-09-28), and neither `trace.py --strict-integrity` nor a plain\n27\t`trace.py` run mentions it.\n28\t\n29\tSR-224 sits on a released rung here (the dial is `DevStg-Boundary`), so an\n30\tadjudication sitting can settle its chain before the check lands.\n31\t\n32\t## Trust\n33\t\n34\tDone-when's commit refusal and standing finding give two cells of the four\n35\tspine registries authority over a commit and over a lane's landing: each\n36\trow's `status`, and its parent references (`sn_refs` on an SR, `sr_refs` on\n37\tan LLR, `verifies` on a TC; `SN-Refs`, `SR-Refs` and `Verifies` as the\n38\treaders name them). The carriers are `docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.toml`\n39\t(its `need` tier), `docs/requirements/system-requirements.toml`,\n40\t`docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.toml` and\n41\t`docs/test/test-cases.toml`, with their legacy forms (`.md` needs, `.csv`\n42\trows), which the same readers resolve. They are hand-edited files on disk, so\n43\tthe guard is owed under\n44\t[PROCESS.md §3, \"When a guard is owed\"](../../../../project-trajectory/PROCESS.md#3-traceability--anti-duplication).\n45\t\n46\t- **Producer:**\n47\t  - *Authoring:* rows are written `Drafted`, as file edits, by Terra's\n48\t    authoring turns, by builder and coordinator sessions and by the owner.\n49\t    `retire.py` deletes a row and writes its record; `migrate_carrier.py`\n50\t    rewrites a whole registry once, at a carrier cutover; `bootstrap.py`\n51\t    scaffolds the `-000` example rows.\n52\t  - *The approval act:* the act turn (an adjudicator turn whose brief\n53\t    `adjudicate_act.py` composes, run through `coordinator_adjudicate.py\n54\t    act`) or the owner edits `status` cells to `Approved` or `Founded` by\n55\t    hand, then runs `intake.py snapshot`. That writes only the copy and the\n56\t    ledger under `docs/archive/last_approved/` (`baseline_snapshot.py`),\n57\t    never a live status cell: the scripted flip is retired (OI-45).\n58\t  - *A demotion:* a hand edit of `status` back to `Drafted`. It writes no\n59\t    copy, since a de-approval is not an act.\n60\t  - Failed-write shapes:\n61\t    - *A malformed file:* a TOML decode error, an explicit empty string, an\n62\t      unquoted dotted table header, or one stem under both carriers.\n63\t    - *A malformed cell:* a row with no `status`, or a word outside\n64\t      `Drafted`, `Approved` and `Founded`; the integrity floor's missing-cell\n65\t      and closed-vocabulary findings already name both. A stray non-UTF-8\n66\t      byte is decoded with replacement by every reader.\n67\t    - *A partial act:* some of an act's flips saved and not others, a child\n68\t      flipped without its parent, or a parent demoted under approved\n69\t      children. The last two are what this row refuses; a flip without its\n70\t      copy, or the reverse, is the snapshot mirror's finding\n71\t      (`acceptance_record.staged_snapshot_findings`).\n72\t    - *A truncated file* (a writer killed mid-write): a decode error in\n73\t      nearly every case, so a malformed file.\n74\t    - *An unstaged edit:* on disk but not in the index. The commit judgement\n75\t      reads the index; the standing finding reads the disk.\n76\t\n77\t- **Consumers** (grep over `project-trajectory/scripts` and\n78\t  `project-trajectory/hooks`):\n79\t  - *The rule's one home:* a pure function in\n80\t    `project-trajectory/scripts/kitlib/spine.py`, beside `is_approved`,\n81\t    `is_founded`, `refs` and `missing_cell_findings`. Given the four tiers'\n82\t    rows, it returns one entry per approved child (`Approved` or `Founded`)\n83\t    citing a parent row that exists and is not approved, naming the child,\n84\t    the parent and both statuses. Its edges are an SR's `SN-Refs` to SNs, an\n85\t    LLR's `SR-Refs` to SRs, and a TC's `Verifies` to SRs and LLRs. Its\n86\t    approval test is the pair `acceptance_record._claims_approval` names\n87\t    today: the pair moves into `kitlib.spine` with the rule and\n88\t    `_claims_approval` binds it, so the rule and the approval-act readers\n89\t    agree on what counts as approved. Not `coherence.py`, whose only caller\n90\t    is `trace.analyze` by its own statement, and not `spine_rules.py`, whose\n91\t    callers are the stage derivation's.\n92\t  - *The commit judgement's one home:*\n93\t    `project-trajectory/scripts/acceptance_record.py`, beside\n94\t    `APPROVAL_ACT_CSVS`, `_commit_parents` and the two-tree readers: one\n95\t    function judging a tree (the index, or a commit) against its parents. It\n96\t    calls the rule on the tree and keeps each entry whose child or parent\n97\t    status moved against a parent. It has a staged form (the index against\n98\t    HEAD, and against MERGE_HEAD during a merge) and a per-commit rider\n99\t    form.\n100\t  - *Callers that only call:*\n101\t    - `project-trajectory/scripts/trace.py` `integrity_sweep`: the standing\n102\t      finding, in `findings.integrity`, so `--strict-integrity` and\n103\t      `--strict` exit 1 on it. `check.py`'s `registry-integrity` step runs it\n104\t      at pre-commit, at every bar, in CI and in the merge slot's bar\n105\t      (`integrate._run_bar`); `traceability` runs `--strict`.\n106\t    - `project-trajectory/scripts/text_then_act.py`: the rider joins\n107\t      `LANE_COMMIT_RIDERS`; `lane_commit_walk` is unchanged.\n108\t    - `project-trajectory/scripts/integrate.py`: `_text_then_act_refusal`,\n109\t      through `_each_lane_commit`, already walks every rider at the merge\n110\t      slot. The rider's lines carry their own remedy, as the adoption\n111\t      record's do, so the slot's code does not change.\n112\t    - `project-trajectory/scripts/check.py`: one new step, a mode in\n113\t      `_text_then_act_mode`'s shape at `STAGE_NEEDS`, named in\n114\t      `BUILTIN_STEP_NAMES` and kept out of `_TRUNK_FRESHNESS_STEPS`, calling\n115\t      the staged form.\n116\t    - `project-trajectory/hooks/pre-commit`: the step joins the batched\n117\t      `--run-steps` list.\n118\t    - `project-trajectory/scripts/trace.py` `llr_status_advisories` (WI-129)\n119\t      retires in the same change. Each case it warns of, an unapproved LLR\n120\t      whose every citing TC is `Approved`, is now an integrity finding on the\n121\t      TC-to-LLR edge.\n122\t  - *Readers checked and unchanged:* `spine_carrier.py`, which supplies the\n123\t    reads and the contract below; `spine_rules.py` and `derive_stage.py`,\n124\t    where a `Drafted` parent already holds its rung open; `census.py`, whose\n125\t    `gap_census` reads orphans and status findings, so the new finding mints\n126\t    no gap row; `adjudicate_act.py` and `coordinator_adjudicate.py`, whose act\n127\t    commit meets the refusal at the hook and the slot; `plan_gate.py`, which\n128\t    walks lane commits with its own judge.\n129\t\n130\t- **Ruling** (drafted for the owner to confirm or overrule):\n131\t  - *What the commit judgement binds to:* one commit's tree and its parents'\n132\t    trees. At pre-commit that is the index against HEAD (and MERGE_HEAD); at\n133\t    the slot, each lane commit against its own parents. Never the working\n134\t    tree, and no history past the parents. It reads the status cells and the\n135\t    parent references only: a parent whose approved text has drifted from\n136\t    its copy still reads approved here, and that drift stays the\n137\t    re-attestation's. A row's status moved when it differs from a parent's,\n138\t    an absent row counting as a value; in a merge, from any parent's, so a\n139\t    violation a merge assembles from two sides is the merge's own. A commit\n140\t    whose name list (read `--no-renames`) touches no carrier of the four\n141\t    registries moved no status and is not read further. A squash landing is\n142\t    judged like any commit: the rule asks what the tree leaves, not who wrote\n143\t    it, so it needs no squash path.\n144\t  - *The standing finding* reads through `spine_carrier.load` and\n145\t    `load_needs` under their existing contract (IF-102): an absent registry\n146\t    is no rows; one that exists and does not parse, carries an explicit\n147\t    empty string or an unquoted dotted id, or exists under both carriers,\n148\t    raises SystemExit naming the file. `--strict-integrity` then exits 1 and\n149\t    `registry-integrity` fails. This is fail-closed already and needs no new\n150\t    code.\n151\t  - *The rule:* a row with no status, or with a word outside the vocabulary,\n152\t    is not approved, so an approved child under it is a finding (and the\n153\t    cell has its own integrity finding). A parent id no row declares is not\n154\t    judged: the orphan rules' `references unknown` finding (`coherence.py`)\n155\t    owns it, as IF-141 already says of unresolved parentage. A cited id\n156\t    outside the four tiers (an IF, B or DA row) is not a parent.\n157\t  - *The commit judgement* reads each registry at a tree through\n158\t    `kitlib.git.git_show` (None when the tree does not list the path; it\n159\t    raises `UnreadableBlob` when the tree lists it and git cannot read it)\n160\t    and `spine_carrier.tier_rows_from_text` (None, never `{}`, for TOML that\n161\t    does not parse). It does not read through\n162\t    `acceptance_record._spine_rows_at`, which takes both failures for an\n163\t    absent registry.\n164\t    - Absent on the commit side: no rows, and its edges are the orphan\n165\t      rules'. Absent on a parent side (the commit adds the registry): every\n166\t      row in it counts as moved.\n167\t    - Listed but unreadable, or not parsing, on the commit side: a refusal\n168\t      line naming the registry and the tree, since whether the commit leaves\n169\t      an approved row under an unapproved parent is unknown.\n170\t    - The same on a parent side: every status on the commit side counts as\n171\t      moved, so the commit's tree is judged in full. A commit that repairs a\n172\t      broken registry is judged on its own rows, and is never refused for\n173\t      its parent's state.\n174\t    - A diff git cannot produce: a refusal line, never a skip, as text then\n175\t      act rules. A commit or parent git cannot read: `_commit_parents`'\n176\t      line. A range the slot cannot list: `_each_lane_commit`'s refusal.\n177\t    - No HEAD yet (the first commit): nothing is judged, as text then act\n178\t      rules; the standing finding reads that tree. Off git or outside a work\n179\t      tree: there is no commit to judge, as for every staged step.\n180\t  - *The step* exits 1 printing the refusal, else 0. A crashed step exits\n181\t    nonzero, which the step runner reads as FAIL, never SKIP; the slot's bar\n182\t    refuses a SKIP.\n183\t\n184\t- **The bound**\n185\t  ([PROCESS.md §6, \"Review threat model\"](../../../../project-trajectory/PROCESS.md#6-review-depth-triage-efficiency)):\n186\t  the promise reaches what supported workflows and the kit's own routes\n187\t  write into the four registries, a hand edit of an authored row included,\n188\t  since rows are authored by hand; hand-edited kit-written state (the\n189\t  approval record under `docs/archive/last_approved/`, a generated view), a\n190\t  hand-chosen name alias (`sr-224` cited for `SR-224`) and a sub-second\n191\t  timing window (a writer killed mid-write, a file rewritten between a\n192\t  reader's two reads of it) lie outside it.\n193\t\n194\t- **The parent set, and the one reader** (coordinator-2026-10-22 D-002, for\n195\t  the owner to confirm or overrule): a TC's parents are the SRs and LLRs its\n196\t  `verifies` cites. An IF row it cites carries no approval status (an\n197\t  interface is approved through the sitting of the row it serves; OI-112's\n198\t  ruling, review-churn plan revision 35), so it is no parent: counting IF\n199\t  rows would make 114 approved TCs here standing findings, demotion their\n200\t  only remedy. The standing finding stays an integrity finding, as Done-when\n201\t  states, and Done-when's first act clears the only standing case here\n202\t  (LLR-281 and TC-291 under SR-224). The commit judgement's two-tree read\n203\t  keeps \"cannot be read\" and \"does not parse\" apart; WI-963's held lane adds\n204\t  that reader (`acceptance_record._tier_reads`), so whichever row lands\n205\t  first carries it as its own behaviour-preserving commit and the other\n206\t  rebases onto it. The plan settles the rest of the draft's open points (the\n207\t  in-lane skill line on a parent \"approved for its current text\", which this\n208\t  check reads by status only; retiring `trace.llr_status_advisories`).\n209\t\n210\t## Done-when\n211\t\n212\t- First, in its own act: SR-224's chain is settled. Either SR-224 is\n213\t  adjudicated for first approval, or LLR-281 and TC-291 return to `Drafted`.\n214\t- A commit that leaves an approved row (`Approved` or `Founded`) under a parent\n215\t  that is not approved is refused, where the commit moved either row's status.\n216\t  An SR waits for every SN it cites, an LLR for every SR, and a TC for every\n217\t  SR and LLR it verifies. It is refused at pre-commit and on each lane commit through\n218\t  the shared lane-commit walk (WI-892).\n219\t- `trace.py --strict-integrity` reports any standing case as an integrity\n220\t  finding, naming the child, the parent and both statuses.\n221\t- `spine-authoring`'s parent-first rule (WI-848) names this check as its\n222\t  enforcer.\n223\t- Its rows (SR-178's chain, or a labelled derived SR if no approved row demands\n224\t  the rule) are authored as one change set and judged in one combined sitting.\n225\t- Tests: each tier pair; `Founded` counts as approved; demoting a parent under\n226\t  an approved child is refused; the standing finding.\n227\t- Review bar: A (one cross-family REVIEW-A).\n228\t- RESYNC_PACK: an entry anchored at a trunk commit. An adopter with an approved\n229\t  child under an unapproved parent sees the new integrity finding at upgrade,\n230\t  and the entry names the two remedies.\n231\t\n232\t## Critique dispositions\n233\t\n234\tScope critique (Codex, 2026-10-22; CHANGES-REQUESTED, findings=3):\n235\t\n236\t- **MAJOR, S2 (Done-when gives on-disk registry cells gate authority with no\n237\t  Trust section): adopted.** `## Trust` now names the carriers and each\n238\t  producer route with its failed-write shapes, the consumers by path with\n239\t  \"The rule's one home: a pure function in\n240\t  `project-trajectory/scripts/kitlib/spine.py`\" and \"The commit judgement's\n241\t  one home: `project-trajectory/scripts/acceptance_record.py`\", and a ruling\n242\t  per consumer, among them \"Listed but unreadable, or not parsing, on the\n243\t  commit side: a refusal line naming the registry and the tree\" and \"A diff\n244\t  git cannot produce: a refusal line, never a skip\".\n245\t- **MAJOR, S4 (no `planmode` while the row changes trace enforcement and\n246\t  commit and lane admission): adopted.** The frontmatter carries\n247\t  `planmode = \"single\"`.\n248\t- **MINOR, S3 (no explicit supported-workflow bound): adopted.** `## Trust`\n249\t  ends with the bound: \"the promise reaches what supported workflows and the\n250\t  kit's own routes write into the four registries, a hand edit of an\n251\t  authored row included, since rows are authored by hand; hand-edited\n252\t  kit-written state (...), a hand-chosen name alias (...) and a sub-second\n253\t  timing window (...) lie outside it.\"\n254\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f031c6a5-ecf0-490f-9890-c7a8a87f92fc","uuid":"478cc77f-b5b4-4bf5-98a1-bbf2784452bc","timestamp":"2026-10-23T04:47:48.145Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Projects\\ai-template.wt\\wi-850\\docs\\work\\active\\wi-850\\WI-850-child-approved-after-parent.md","content":"+++\nid = \"WI-850\"\ntitle = \"A spine row is approved only after the rows it hangs from are\"\nworkstream = \"process\"\nsr_refs = [\"SR-178\"]\nspecref = \"docs/plans/2026-10-07-wi841-retro/PROPOSAL.md\"\nneeds = [\"WI-892\"]\nbuildtier = \"medium\"\nsafety_class = \"ordinary\"\npriority = 5\nplanmode = \"single\"\n+++\n\n## Context\n\nRe-homed 2026-10-10 (D-015): the shared lane-commit walk this row rides\nmoved from WI-828 (closed partial) to WI-892, which landed the same day.\nOI-113 was ruled (d), won't fix, on 2026-10-14, and WI-893 was cancelled: no\nhook walks a hand merge, and the merge slot is the sanctioned landing path.\n\nFiled by hand on 2026-10-07 from the WI-841 retrospective (proposal §7.2,\nrow P3), carrying the owner's ruling 4 of 2026-10-07\n(`docs/log.d/2026-10-07-wi841-retro-owner-rulings.md`): a child is approved\nonly after its parent. The owner expected the check to exist. It does